  "note": "Triennial election of Omagh Urban District Council, January 1929. Nominations were received by Mr. W. J. Corker, returning officer, on Saturday 5 January 1929. North Ward and West Ward were filled without a contest. For the seven South Ward seats the seven Nationalist and Labour candidates selected at the Nationalist convention were nominated, and an eighth nomination was lodged by Mr. James M'Gillion, a retiring member not selected at the convention (the Belfast News-Letter, 7 January 1929, p. 12, called him an Independent Nationalist); Mr. M'Gillion withdrew on the Monday evening and the other seven were returned unopposed, so there was no poll in the town (Ulster Herald, 12 January 1929, p. 5). The paper gives the group label 'Nationalist and Labour' for the ward's candidates, not a label for each, so none is recorded. Returned unopposed."
